1028
00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:58.540
And I loved that detail then, and I love it now.

1029
00:40:58.680 --> 00:40:59.320
I loved it.

1030
00:40:59.320 --> 00:41:04.160
I remember reading one one time in the back of a scene, and there had been a really big

1031
00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:08.160
snow storm, and I was reading about the snow in Pennsylvania.

1032
00:41:08.400 --> 00:41:08.620
Yeah.

1033
00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:09.780
All right.

1034
00:41:09.940 --> 00:41:12.040
Well, things are about to really heat up.

1035
00:41:12.160 --> 00:41:13.960
There's going to be some tension.

1036
00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:14.500
Yeah.

1037
00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:15.260
Should we take a break?

1038
00:41:15.380 --> 00:41:17.080
Let's take a break, and then we're going to get into it.

1039
00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:17.500
All right.

1040
00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:29.380
We are back, but I'm the only one talking because Angela is being silent.

1041
00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:32.140
Angela, I need to talk about your hair today.

1042
00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:32.900
I'm sorry.

1043
00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:36.980
It is a hot mess.

1044
00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:38.280
What happened to you?

1045
00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:41.840
I showered this morning before I took Isabel to school.

1046
00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:42.140
Yeah.

1047
00:41:42.260 --> 00:41:44.400
And my goal was to show up here clean.

1048
00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:45.620
You are clean.

1049
00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:46.740
That's as far as I got.

1050
00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:48.680
So I got Isabel to school on time.

1051
00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:50.040
I was really happy about that.

1052
00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:54.220
And I drove here with wet hair, and I was like, oh, I'll freeze.

1053
00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:55.580
I will freeze there.

1054
00:41:55.580 --> 00:41:56.460
It's so cold.

1055
00:41:56.460 --> 00:42:00.320
So I kind of put my window down, and I let the wind kind of dry my hair.

1056
00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:01.520
This is explaining it all.

1057
00:42:01.780 --> 00:42:06.780
And now I have this wind-dried hair from the freeway.

1058
00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:08.140
Yeah.

1059
00:42:08.420 --> 00:42:08.860
You do.

1060
00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:09.400
I do.

1061
00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:10.500
I do, guys.

1062
00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:12.380
And you know what?

1063
00:42:12.380 --> 00:42:14.300
I feel fantastic.

1064
00:42:15.560 --> 00:42:17.840
I, you know, I love it.

1065
00:42:18.100 --> 00:42:19.000
You know, I love it.

1066
00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:21.380
I look like Medusa a little bit.

1067
00:42:21.980 --> 00:42:22.560
You know.

1068
00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:25.620
But it's dry, and I'm not cold, and I'm clean.

1069
00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:27.980
And I got Isabel to school on time.

1070
00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:28.440
Goals.

1071
00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:29.280
Okay.

1072
00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:35.400
When we come back, Michael busts into the women's meeting to tell Jan that the warehouse

1073
00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:36.700
wants to start a union.

1074
00:42:37.040 --> 00:42:41.140
And Jan is like, Michael, no, there cannot be a union.

1075
00:42:41.240 --> 00:42:42.820
You didn't agree to this, right, Michael?

1076
00:42:42.920 --> 00:42:47.020
Like, the men in the warehouse weren't chanting, Michael, Michael, just two minutes ago, right?

1077
00:42:47.180 --> 00:42:48.920
And he's like, no, I definitely.

1078
00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:52.260
He says something that would have made me go nuts.

1079
00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:54.860
He says, okay, don't be hysterical.

1080
00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:55.400
Oh, yeah.

1081
00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:57.660
That's like you need to calm down.

1082
00:42:57.840 --> 00:42:59.460
That's like one of those kind of phrases.

1083
00:42:59.680 --> 00:43:00.300
Yeah, that's a trigger.

1084
00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:02.280
That's a trigger for sure.

1085
00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:05.180
And Pam, your reaction is hilarious.

1086
00:43:05.180 --> 00:43:07.620
Once again, you have all of these facial reactions.

1087
00:43:08.100 --> 00:43:10.520
I had so much fun during this episode.

1088
00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:14.840
Well, when we go down to the warehouse again, I loved this moment.

1089
00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:20.700
The scene when the sales guys are unpacking the truck, and Ryan says,

1090
00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:24.300
you guys, we could get this job quicker if we form an assembly line.

1091
00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:28.620
And Stanley cuts him off and says, this is a run out the clock situation.

1092
00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:28.940
Yeah.

1093
00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:30.420
Just like upstairs.

1094
00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:32.040
I'm going to break it down to you, kid.

1095
00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:33.400
This is how we're doing this.

1096
00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:37.440
We are not going to hypothesize on how to be more efficient.

1097
00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:39.680
This is just what gets done gets done.

1098
00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:40.620
Run out the clock.

1099
00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:41.980
Run out the clock situation.

1100
00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:45.420
I was watching this with my daughter, and she goes, Mom, what does he mean?

1101
00:43:45.420 --> 00:43:49.740
And I said, Isabel, he wants to do the bare minimum, the bare minimum.

1102
00:43:49.940 --> 00:43:51.780
She was like, I kind of get that.

1103
00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:53.240
I get it.

1104
00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:56.140
I've been in a lot of run out the clock situations.

1105
00:43:56.940 --> 00:44:01.980
So when we go back upstairs, Jan starts explaining sports terMs.

1106
00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:03.140
Sports metaphors.

1107
00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:03.820
To the women.

1108
00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:05.620
It's how women might be left out in the workplace.

1109
00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:07.640
I'm like, is this the 1950s?

1110
00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:08.080
I know.

1111
00:44:08.260 --> 00:44:09.500
This was so dated.

1112
00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:15.220
This felt a little bit like it was out of some manual from 1953 or something.

1113
00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:20.020
Well, at this point, Kelly pretends like she doesn't know what second base means.

1114
00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:23.640
She says, okay, well, Michael said he got to second base with you.

1115
00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:25.240
Does that mean you closed a sale?

1116
00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:27.500
Is that what second base means?

1117
00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:28.420
Guess what happened?

1118
00:44:28.420 --> 00:44:30.600
And this is a real rare thing.

1119
00:44:30.740 --> 00:44:33.280
This is like seeing an albino rhino in the wild.

1120
00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:33.720
What?

1121
00:44:34.140 --> 00:44:35.300
Angela laughs.

1122
00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:36.000
Yes.

1123
00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:39.020
15 minutes, 10 seconds.

1124
00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:42.440
Fan question from Jeff Ackerman.

1125
00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:43.380
He pointed it out.

1126
00:44:43.540 --> 00:44:49.300
There is a cutaway shot of Pam and Angela after Kelly plays dumb and asks Jan what second base means.

1127
00:44:49.480 --> 00:44:50.760
Was that scripted?

1128
00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:53.420
Or was it so funny you couldn't keep a straight face?

1129
00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:55.760
That is me and Angela laughing.

1130
00:44:56.000 --> 00:44:56.760
We're laughing.

1131
00:44:56.940 --> 00:44:57.360
We're laughing.

1132
00:44:57.360 --> 00:45:06.040
But I also knew that my character really thought that Jan was just like, well, she says she looks whorish.

1133
00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:11.200
So she clearly doesn't think highly of Jan and judges her.

1134
00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:16.580
And so for Kelly to call her out, Angela thoroughly enjoyed that moment.

1135
00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:18.720
I have an observation about this episode.

1136
00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:19.280
Oh, yes.

1137
00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:22.620
And I'm going to get right up on my soapbox and shout it.

1138
00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:23.860
Here we go.

1139
00:45:23.860 --> 00:45:32.380
While re-watching this episode, I noticed that the people who are hardest on Jan and her ambition are other women.

1140
00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:32.760
Yeah.

1141
00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:33.820
Other women.

1142
00:45:34.140 --> 00:45:36.440
Angela calls her a slut for the way she dresses.

1143
00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:39.460
Pam is constantly rolling her eyes at her.

1144
00:45:39.640 --> 00:45:44.640
And then Kelly shames her slash embarrasses her for making out with Michael.

1145
00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:48.400
Then they lay into her for not being a mother.

1146
00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:49.280
Yeah.

1147
00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:55.200
Oftentimes it is other women who make ambition hardest for women.

1148
00:45:55.620 --> 00:46:02.560
I wondered if BJ intended to point this out or if it was a happy accident because you know what?

1149
00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:05.080
Jan is so excited to help Pam.

1150
00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:08.740
She wants to help these other women rise up.

1151
00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:09.980
She is so excited.

1152
00:46:10.340 --> 00:46:16.920
Now, listen, I think that it is totally valid when Phyllis says, I'm happy with my choices.

1153
00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:17.420
Yeah.

1154
00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:18.620
We don't all need to be you.

1155
00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:23.360
Not all women or all people need to be ambitious or share ambitions.

1156
00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:30.140
But they are really hard on her and unaccepting of her for wanting to be her.

1157
00:46:30.860 --> 00:46:33.540
Like, Phyllis is like, I'm going to be me.

1158
00:46:33.940 --> 00:46:36.180
But then they're kind of hard on Jan for being Jan.

1159
00:46:36.440 --> 00:46:40.300
Well, I think Phyllis is probably the most fair of everyone.

1160
00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:40.860
True.

1161
00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:48.360
She's really happy with her life choices and stands by them and is unapologetic and has a very happy life.

1162
00:46:48.540 --> 00:46:54.360
And I don't think that she wishes for Jan not to have anything.

1163
00:46:54.580 --> 00:46:57.000
I think Phyllis is happy that Jan has chosen her path.

1164
00:46:57.120 --> 00:47:00.900
But everyone else in the room is snarky towards Jan.

1165
00:47:01.040 --> 00:47:01.160
Yeah.

1166
00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:01.680
They are.

1167
00:47:01.920 --> 00:47:06.380
And I think one of the things I love about our friendship,

1168
00:47:06.700 --> 00:47:15.800
and you and I both have like a group of female friends who are really about supporting one another and building each other up and encouraging each other.

1169
00:47:15.980 --> 00:47:18.940
And you really need those women in your life.

1170
00:47:19.060 --> 00:47:19.220
Yeah.

1171
00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:20.560
You really do.

1172
00:47:20.560 --> 00:47:21.120
Yeah.

1173
00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:28.660
Because that really, the momentum that women give one another is really the momentum that moves us forward.

1174
00:47:28.880 --> 00:47:29.280
Mm-hmm.

1175
00:47:29.780 --> 00:47:30.280
Mostly.

1176
00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:32.100
All right, Jenna, step on down.

1177
00:47:32.380 --> 00:47:32.900
I'm stepping down.

1178
00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:33.480
Step down.

1179
00:47:33.620 --> 00:47:34.120
I like it.

1180
00:47:34.160 --> 00:47:35.100
I like what you had to say.

1181
00:47:35.380 --> 00:47:35.740
All right.

1182
00:47:35.860 --> 00:47:36.860
I'm off my soapbox.

1183
00:47:36.880 --> 00:47:44.000
Well, here's a juxtaposition to your soapbox moment, which I thought was really just well said.

1184
00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:44.560
Thanks.

1185
00:47:44.780 --> 00:47:47.400
Here's at 15 minutes, 59 seconds.

1186
00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:47.700
Yep.

1187
00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:50.260
Michael puts packing peanuts into a fan.

1188
00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:51.180
What a dick.

1189
00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:53.220
That's what I had to say.

1190
00:47:53.400 --> 00:47:54.520
That's my deep thought.

1191
00:47:55.600 --> 00:48:00.120
Well, Jan gets so annoyed that she goes down to the warehouse.

1192
00:48:00.440 --> 00:48:00.600
Yeah.

1193
00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:06.860
And this is when Jan tells the warehouse that if they unionize, they're all going to lose their jobs.

1194
00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:07.160
Right.

1195
00:48:07.380 --> 00:48:09.660
Very, very, very anti-union.

1196
00:48:09.660 --> 00:48:11.940
Yes, and I read, actually,

1197
00:48:12.080 --> 00:48:28.480
that this episode has been used in law classes that deal with companies who have unions and who want to form unions and that it has been used to show the pressure and the hard road that they can have in those moments.

1198
00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:33.360
All right, guys, we're going to get into some sad times now for Jim and Pam.

1199
00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:34.140
Ooh.

1200
00:48:34.560 --> 00:48:35.120
Oh, boy.

1201
00:48:35.380 --> 00:48:35.540
Yeah.

1202
00:48:35.960 --> 00:48:37.640
Jim comes upstairs to make a phone call.

1203
00:48:37.900 --> 00:48:45.840
Pam excuses herself from her meeting, and she very excitedly tells Jim about this internship opportunity in graphic design.

1204
00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:49.220
Jim adorably encourages her immediately.

1205
00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:51.480
He's like, well, what are the details?

1206
00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:52.340
I think you should do it.

1207
00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:53.080
I know.

1208
00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:54.920
And she just lights up.

1209
00:48:55.100 --> 00:49:00.580
She needed someone to give her permission in a way to go for this.

1210
00:49:00.800 --> 00:49:00.960
Yeah.

1211
00:49:01.060 --> 00:49:02.540
And Jim immediately did.

1212
00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:02.960
Yes.

1213
00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:03.720
Because he's her soulmate.

1214
00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:04.300
Yes.

1215
00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:10.420
So you kind of get the sense she's going to go for it, but then you see a scene.

1216
00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:11.600
It's a spy shot.

1217
00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:12.520
There's no dialogue.

1218
00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:14.040
She's holding the pamphlet.

1219
00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:19.980
She's telling Roy about the program, and it's very clear that he is not supportive.

1220
00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:21.600
He sees it as a waste of time.

1221
00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:26.340
So we cut to this Pam talking head, and she starts talking about this house with a terrace.

1222
00:49:26.840 --> 00:49:28.540
Oh, this house with a terrace.

1223
00:49:28.760 --> 00:49:31.940
This metaphor broke my heart.

1224
00:49:32.260 --> 00:49:32.700
Yeah.

1225
00:49:32.940 --> 00:49:38.780
She says she was a little girl, and she read this book, and there was a house with a terrace with flowers.

1226
00:49:39.440 --> 00:49:43.880
And she describes it in detail, and she says, I just always wanted that.

1227
00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:46.920
But, you know, it's just, you know.

1228
00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:49.920
They don't even have those kind of houses in Scranton.

1229
00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:53.820
Well, this was based on a story of mine.

1230
00:49:55.120 --> 00:49:56.800
BJ came up to me on the set.

1231
00:49:56.940 --> 00:49:58.180
We were working on a different episode.

1232
00:49:58.180 --> 00:50:04.460
He was writing this episode, and he came up to me, and he said, Jenna, I've got this moment I'm trying to write.

1233
00:50:04.940 --> 00:50:15.060
Was there ever a dream that you had as a little girl that has gone unrealized because it seemed unrealistic to you?

1234
00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:17.200
He said it can be really simple, though.

1235
00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:18.720
It's a little girl's dream.

1236
00:50:19.620 --> 00:50:21.280
And I said yes.

1237
00:50:21.540 --> 00:50:22.980
How smart is that, though?

1238
00:50:23.120 --> 00:50:23.440
I know.

1239
00:50:23.600 --> 00:50:24.700
BJ is so smart.

1240
00:50:24.800 --> 00:50:26.100
Isn't that such a great question?

1241
00:50:26.200 --> 00:50:27.160
It's so wonderful.

1242
00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:31.420
Writers, most of their job is just asking the right questions.

1243
00:50:31.740 --> 00:50:33.080
BJ is so smart.

1244
00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:35.020
Okay, so what your answer was?

1245
00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:36.160
My answer was yes.

1246
00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:39.040
When I was younger, I read this book.

1247
00:50:39.260 --> 00:50:45.700
And in the book, there was a girl who moved into a new house with her family, and the house had a tower.

1248
00:50:46.680 --> 00:50:55.840
Sort of like a castle might, it was this tower room on the side of the house, and there was one set of stairs that went up to it.

1249
00:50:55.940 --> 00:51:01.100
And so there was this one circular room on the third floor of their home.

1250
00:51:01.100 --> 00:51:07.020
And this was her space that she declared was hers to sit and think and create.

1251
00:51:08.120 --> 00:51:12.820
And I had such a clear vision of what this tower room was.

1252
00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:20.200
And I remember thinking to myself, one day, I'm going to have a house with a circular tower room where I will create.

1253
00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:25.700
And I've never had it, and I never got it, because who builds houses like that?

1254
00:51:25.980 --> 00:51:28.220
I think this dream, do not let it go.

1255
00:51:28.400 --> 00:51:28.800
I think.

1256
00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:29.720
Maybe one day.

1257
00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:35.040
One day, maybe, you know, they'll be like, you know, Jenna Fisher, she lives in a tower.

1258
00:51:36.060 --> 00:51:41.800
She has a very normal-looking house, and then next to it, she has built this tower.

1259
00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:43.040
In Missouri.

1260
00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:44.720
It's very strange.

1261
00:51:45.400 --> 00:51:51.740
So BJ ran with that story, and that was, he adapted it, and he made it a terrace with flowers.

1262
00:51:53.020 --> 00:51:56.260
But that was a little bit of collaboration there.

1263
00:51:56.260 --> 00:51:59.160
I think it's such a sweet story, and then it broke my heart.

1264
00:51:59.460 --> 00:52:01.860
Well, Pam then is in the kitchen with Jim.

1265
00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:05.160
Her talking head is kind of broken up by this scene in the kitchen.

1266
00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:10.780
And she's saying to Jim, Jim is like, wait, you're not going to take the internship?

1267
00:52:10.780 --> 00:52:16.320
And she's like, well, you know, like Roy said, there's no guarantee it's going to lead to anything anyway.

1268
00:52:16.580 --> 00:52:17.680
He's like, Roy said that?

1269
00:52:18.060 --> 00:52:20.960
And then Pam flips a switch.

1270
00:52:21.060 --> 00:52:22.680
She's like, oh, do you have something to say?

1271
00:52:22.820 --> 00:52:25.240
I was like, oh, dang, here we go.

1272
00:52:25.460 --> 00:52:26.080
I know.

1273
00:52:26.600 --> 00:52:27.400
She's hurt.

1274
00:52:27.600 --> 00:52:28.420
She's hurt.

1275
00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:30.640
And it's not Jim's fault.

1276
00:52:30.660 --> 00:52:32.360
He's speaking truth, you know?

1277
00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:32.520
I know.

1278
